	Consider the following passage from Nowak, which nicely crystallizes both the substance and the spirit of his argument's starting place:

	"If you travel to a foreign country where you do not speak the local language, you may be able to get others to see that you are patient or impatient, generous or not generous, but all of the fineness of grain that colors your interactions with people in your mother tongue is lost. Your wit, your tenderness, and your grace depend on the words you choose as much as they do on your underlying dispositions where non-linguistic behavior is concerned. There is a sense of 'personality', in other words, that is inherently social and that requires not just that you be a certain way, but that others *recognize* you as so being." (9)

	Nowak begins with the clear and compelling observation of the felt difference between what it is like to speak one's first language, and what it is like to speak any other. We are familiar with the fact that people who speak more than one language report a distinct sense in which certain expressions or ways of stating or coloring a thought cannot really be translated out of whichever language they originated from. When someone grows up with a home language that they end up becoming more fluent in than any other, they will thereby experience a discrepancy in a sense of communicative ability when speaking another language.
	Nowak also observes, as is intuitive and evident from our own experience, that our conversational choices in dialogue play a central part in our coming to be known or perceived by others as having certain traits, or exhibiting this-or-that kind of behavior, which then comes to be entered into the register of how others bear us in mind, categorize us, and respond to us. This is just to say that one's conversational choices heavily contribute to the character of one's social identity. So if a speaker encounters a difference in the conversational constraints and options she is able to avail herself of across languages, this in turn, results in differences in the resolution at which a speaker can get nuance in the public register of her personality, her manner, and her individuality in articulating her thoughts, to come across. In this way, when a speaker is impaired in her communicative ability, relative to her fluency in her first language, she is likewise relatively impaired with respect to her ability to exert more detailed control over the character of her social identity.
	Upon accepting this line of reasoning, it is expected that these considerations will lead us to acknowledge that there is value in speakers maintaining access to their first languages, and to ask whether and to what extent this access should therefore be protected. Nowak does not trace any direct implications from his argument to any distinct policy or course of action. But, taken generally, perhaps the clear ideal that can be abstracted here is this: The world ought to be such that speakers are always capable of entering and leaving any arenas of linguistic-social impairment (i.e. conversational space outside of their first languages) freely. However extreme or conservative one might be with this ideal is then left up to future discussion. Nowak himself take the absolute minimum to be established that we should "...seriously consider the measures we might take to guantee that people are not cut off from their linguistic communities" (9).